WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:04.459
What Happened on December 26, 2004? One Sunday morning in Thailand, two brothers, eight and twelve years

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:12.112
old, set out for a day at the beach. It was a clear morning with the promise

3
00:00:12.113 --> 00:00:20.191
of more good weather ahead, and the boys were looking forward to swimming and playing. They

4
00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:29.113
had no notion that, eight hundred miles away, a huge undersea earthquake was sending devastation their way.

5
00:00:29.114 --> 00:00:36.113
The boys' village, Khao Lak, was a village very much like others that bordered the Indian Ocean.

6
00:00:36.114 --> 00:00:44.845
Many of its residents made their livelihood from fishing, or working in the hotels visited by thousands

7
00:00:44.846 --> 00:00:52.352
of foreign tourists each year. The morning was unusually quiet, but as the boys approached the beach,

8
00:00:52.353 --> 00:01:00.345
they heard a strange roaring sound. Suddenly they spotted an enormous wave, unlike anything they'd ever

9
00:01:00.346 --> 00:01:11.905
seen, hurtling toward them! Thinking fast, the brothers scaled nearby trees just before the deadly wall of

10
00:01:11.906 --> 00:01:21.894
water exploded across the shore, swamping the beach and the area around it. From the treetops, the

11
00:01:21.895 --> 00:01:29.726
boys watched in shock and horror as the people below tried desperately to cling to tree trunks

12
00:01:29.727 --> 00:01:42.795
and branches to save themselves. Beneath their feet, the rushing waters swept away cars, boats, parts of

13
00:01:42.796 --> 00:01:51.384
buildings, and people's belongings. The boys waited until things seemed calm. Then they dropped down and started

14
00:01:51.385 --> 00:02:02.879
for home, traveling across a frightening, dripping landscape filled with mud, debris, and injured people. The

15
00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.118
exhausted boys finally found their mother, who hugged them fiercely. Their house was gone, and their father

16
00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:10.778
was missing. Still the boys and their mother were among the fortunate ones: they had survived while

17
00:02:10.779 --> 00:02:21.519
hundreds of thousands of other people along the coastlines had perished. A Tsunami Strikes The Indian Ocean

18
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:26.670
tsunami was one of the worst disasters ever to occur on Earth. The tsunami was set off

19
00:02:26.671 --> 00:02:34.007
by a powerful underwater earthquake, one that came without warning, and found the people of the

20
00:02:34.008 --> 00:02:44.684
Indian Ocean basin completely unprepared. The tsunami first reached Banda Aceh, a region in northern Indonesia. Other

21
00:02:44.685 --> 00:02:53.164
countries close to the earthquake’s starting point included the Maldives, Sri Lanka, and Thailand. The tsunami eventually

22
00:02:53.165 --> 00:02:59.893
ranged as far as the east coasts of India and Africa. Within hours it had wreaked havoc

23
00:02:59.894 --> 00:03:08.300
on two continents and thousands of lives. Up to 230,000 people are believed to have perished in

24
00:03:08.301 --> 00:03:14.808
the tsunami. At least 40,000 more went missing. Entire towns were destroyed; hundreds of thousands of

25
00:03:14.809 --> 00:03:22.204
people lost their homes. The tsunami even reshaped the landscape, swamping tiny islands and coral reefs and

26
00:03:22.205 --> 00:03:24.617
flooding crops and farmland. Altogether the tsunami caused approximately twelve billion dollars of property damage. Recovering from

27
00:03:24.618 --> 00:03:34.880
all these losses, and rebuilding homes and lives, has been a long and difficult process. What Caused

28
00:03:34.881 --> 00:03:41.699
the Tsunami? Tsunamis are giant waves caused by violent movement in the seabed, which comes from an

29
00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:51.122
earthquake, an undersea volcano, or a landslide. Most tsunamis remain at sea and do not generate

30
00:03:51.123 --> 00:04:02.618
giant waves that crash onto shorelines. A very powerful earthquake, however, can send waves hurtling hundreds of

31
00:04:02.619 --> 00:04:07.759
miles across the ocean and as far as a mile onto land. This was true of the

32
00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.926
2004 earthquake. It registered 9.2 on the Richter scale, a system used by scientists to classify the

33
00:04:10.927 --> 00:04:18.325
intensity of earthquakes. Vibrations from this quake were detected all around the world. How Do Tsunamis Trigger

34
00:04:18.326 --> 00:04:25.928
Killer Waves? Earth's crust is made up of giant sections called tectonic plates that fit together like

35
00:04:25.929 --> 00:04:35.502
a jigsaw puzzle. The edges of these plates meet at fault lines, which are great cracks

36
00:04:35.503 --> 00:04:42.994
in the crust where earthquakes most often occur. When one tectonic plate suddenly slips beneath another, a

37
00:04:42.995 --> 00:04:50.565
tremendous amount of energy is released, and this energy is transferred to the water. Imagine dropping a

38
00:04:50.566 --> 00:05:03.643
very large rock into a bathtub and watching the water splash outward from that center point. The

39
00:05:03.644 --> 00:05:06.223
water has been displaced and needs somewhere to go—in this case, all over the bathroom floor! In

40
00:05:06.224 --> 00:05:12.469
an ocean basin, the principle is basically the same: the displaced water moves outward from the

41
00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:17.967
earthquake’s starting point, or epicenter, sending out powerful waves in all directions. Help from around the World

42
00:05:17.968 --> 00:05:27.372
Immediately after the Indian Ocean tsunami, assistance poured into the affected areas. At Khao Lak, Thai military

43
00:05:27.373 --> 00:05:37.273
aircraft brought medical supplies and food. There, and in other locations around Asia, doctors, nurses, and ordinary

44
00:05:37.274 --> 00:05:44.354
citizens arrived to help people who were injured or sick. Relief workers brought food, water, and clothing,

45
00:05:44.355 --> 00:05:49.346
and set up shelters and medical facilities to comfort people left homeless and grieving. Many tourists

46
00:05:49.347 --> 00:05:57.183
stayed to help. Teams created computer databases to try to track the missing people. Tents were provided

47
00:05:57.184 --> 00:06:10.315
for families while temporary housing was constructed. Millions of people who had just celebrated Christmas with their

48
00:06:10.316 --> 00:06:12.815
families viewed the devastation on TV and in newspapers. Moved by the plight of victims who had

49
00:06:12.816 --> 00:06:19.835
lost homes and family members, many individuals donated money, food, clothing, and basic supplies. Help came from

50
00:06:19.836 --> 00:06:29.240
all over the globe. Tsunami Warning Systems After the Indian Ocean disaster, many people wondered how

51
00:06:29.241 --> 00:06:44.653
to prevent devastation from future tsunamis. In 2005 a United Nations conference in Kobe, Japan, led to

52
00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:51.217
the establishment of the International Early Warning Program (IEWP). Modeled after the Pacific Tsunami Warning System in

53
00:06:51.218 --> 00:06:56.801
Hawaii, the IEWP is made up of 25 undersea stations and 3 deep-sea sensors that sit on

54
00:06:56.802 --> 00:07:02.283
the ocean floor in areas where earthquakes often occur. These devices relay information to 26 national tsunami

55
00:07:02.284 --> 00:07:11.698
information centers. Greater awareness and understanding about tsunamis can also help save lives. People can be

56
00:07:11.699 --> 00:07:16.958
educated to look for and respond correctly to warning signs. For instance, just before the Indian Ocean

57
00:07:16.959 --> 00:07:23.932
tsunami struck, people on the coast observed that the water was suddenly sucked back out to sea.

58
00:07:23.933 --> 00:07:33.426
Rocks were exposed, and fish flopped on the wide plain of mud. Many people were fascinated by

59
00:07:33.427 --> 00:07:35.418
this occurrence, and stayed to watch rather than run for higher ground. If people had recognized this

60
00:07:35.419 --> 00:07:40.261
warning sign, they would have known it was time to evacuate. This awareness could have saved their

61
00:07:40.262 --> 00:07:47.172
lives. Hope for the Future The 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami was a terrible tragedy. Hundreds of

62
00:07:47.173 --> 00:07:52.669
thousands of people died, and many others suffered devastating injuries and losses. The lives of the two

63
00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:59.233
brothers who played on the beach that morning were changed forever. They lost their home, and much

64
00:07:59.234 --> 00:08:04.325
worse, they lost their father, who was never found. Still this violent tsunami has taught us many

65
00:08:04.326 --> 00:08:12.976
valuable lessons. The 2004 tsunami showed us how important it is to continue researching tsunamis and developing

66
00:08:12.977 --> 00:08:17.640
ways to forecast them. We have also seen how critical it is to warn people of

67
00:08:17.641 --> 00:08:24.211
their approach. And we have found new ways to help one another when a disaster of great

68
00:08:24.212 --> 00:08:31.721
magnitude strikes. This brings hope that the massive devastation of the 2004 tsunami will never be repeated.

